Before getting into examples, let's prime the intuition.

Pseudo-keyword
:   Usage of `dsl` looks like a keyword, even though it's actually imported!  That's something you'll see elsewhere in CMK, and besides merely keeping the root namespace tidy, it's keeping the grammar itself clean and consistent with the "[extensible language]({{_}}/cmk/paradigm/#language-properties)" goals.

As a Metaclass
:   It's no coincidence that usage of `dsl` resembles [usage of `class`]({{_}}/cmk/banana/#banana-cooked), and these are in fact siblings in the [compiler's tower]({{_}}/cmk/compiler).  Another way to put it: just as the `class` keyword creates **classes** and *not* instances, the `dsl` keyword creates **languages** and does *not* create polyglots / code objects.

Language-Kind and Language-Shapes
:   The last section begins to show that we need a bit of terminology here for a clean mental model.  The concept of a "kind" is [described here]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#kinds) in more detail, but let's summarize.  An "instance" of the `dsl` metaclass is a language or a language-kind.  We prefer "kind" since thinking of it as instance risks missing the point: the instance itself is *still a constructor*.  We think of the internal code-block as a "shape", because there's really no general guarantee that it qualifies as something more specific (e.g. a runnable or a callable). Language-kind's are a specification that describes how shapes will reach a guest interpreter.

{{ macros.section(3, "Simple Example", id="dsl-examples") }}

To keep it simple for the first example, let's look at wrapping the `bc` tool.  (If you're not familiar with it, this little guy is a simple calculator that's been baked into most linux distributions forever.)

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/dsl-bclang.cmk', github=github)}}

Above we see that the *easy* way for a shape to reach a guest interpreter is to make your DSL [machine-backed]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#machines), i.e. providing kwargs for `entrypoint`, `img`, or `machine`.   After that, the DSL fragment is *callable* and can delegate invocation.  Given an `img`, the shape runs in a container, given an `entrypoint`, there is a custom interpreter, etc.


{{ macros.section(3, "Builtin DSLs", id="builtin-dsls") }}

Two guest languages ship in the box, reached qualified as `dsl.jqlang` / `dsl.awklang`.

{{ macros.section(4, "dsl.jqlang", id="jqlang") }}

{{ cmkmath.imath("`dsl.jqlang` @NAME (|..jq program..|)") }} wraps a `jq` program into a callable JSON builder: `NAME(..)` runs it over stdin (any extra words become `jq` flags), and `NAME.locals()` feeds the recipe's target-locals instead.  An anonymous single-use form {{ cmkmath.imath("`dsl.jqlang`(|..|).locals()") }} works too.  It is how `mk.stat` builds its report; the [Banana Forms]({{_}}/cmk/banana) page walks the whole family.

The demo below puts this to work: it creates jq programs as first-class fragments, gives them a callable form, and composes them with a closed pipe algebra, which is exactly the compile-time metaprogramming DSLs are built for.

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/jqlang.cmk', github=github)}}

{{ macros.section(4, "dsl.awklang", id="awklang") }}

{{ cmkmath.imath("`dsl.awklang` @NAME (|..awk program..|)") }} creates an awk stage or utility from the block.  This is the same machinery the [compiler stages]({{_}}/cmk/pragmas/#pragma-compiler_pre_post) are built from, so `awklang` lets a plugin ship a preprocessor pass.  The raw `dsl.awklang(def=<block> run=<tgt> stem=<var>)` form additionally creates a stdin runner target.

