{{ macros.section(3, "Module DSLs", id="module-dsls") }}

Other DSLs ship as importable modules rather than in the box.  Each loads on demand and wraps a real toolchain, so an inline fragment becomes a callable that builds and runs the guest language.

{{ macros.admonition("warning", "Optional Plugin / Module", "The DSLs in this section (`dsl.python`, `dsl.rust`, `dsl.golang`) are non-core plugins, not part of always-loaded `compose.mk`; load each via the [plugin system](" ~ mkdocs.site_relative_url ~ "/plugins/overview).  All need a docker daemon: instance bodies build and run inside a container, and nothing is installed on the host.") }}

{{ macros.section(4, "python", id="python") }}

The `dsl.python` kind wraps a real interpreter.  The demo builds a **Python-scripting** DSL: {{ cmkmath.imath("python @NAME (|..python..|)") }} wraps `python3`, so a task that is fussy in `jq` or shell (arithmetic, f-strings, stdlib) becomes a few lines of Python.  Instances are callable like a built-in: call one to run the script (extra words become arguments), or stream a stdin pipe through it.

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/dsl-python.cmk', github=github)}}

{{ macros.section(4, "dsl.rust", id="rust") }}

This is where the fragment abstraction earns its keep: `dsl.rust` wraps a *compiler*, not an interpreter, so a single inline block stands in for a whole `cargo` project and still presents as one callable.

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/rust.cmk', github=github)}}

{{ macros.section(4, "dsl.golang", id="golang") }}

Same idea, different toolchain.  The point isn't Go specifically; swapping the guest compiler is just a plugin import, and nothing downstream of the callable has to change.

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/golang.cmk', github=github)}}

{{ macros.section(3, "Language Protocol", id="language-protocol", protocols=['Language']) }}

Beyond *running* a guest language, a DSL can also *reflect* on it.  A language kind that conforms to the [`Language`](#language-protocols) protocol exposes `__symbols__`, `__blocks__`, and `__tokens__`: structural views over the concrete syntax of the guest code an instance carries, so a fragment can list its own declarations, balanced blocks, and identifiers.

Reflection here is *structural*, not semantic.  It reads the concrete syntax without a full parse or any execution, which keeps it cheap and language-parametric.  The default provider is [comby](https://comby.dev), a structural matcher reached through the `lang.parser` interface; a `matcher` hint (`.go`, `.py`, `.rs`, ...) selects the guest grammar, and comby is only the swappable implementation *behind* that interface.

A kind opts in through the [`ifaces=`]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#protocols) slot, mixing the protocol onto an existing language.  The demo below does this for Go: `dsl.golang` both builds/runs a fragment *and* reflects it onto its own symbols and tokens.  Each kind also picks a *grammar family* that supplies the comby patterns behind `__symbols__` and `__blocks__`: brace-delimited languages (Go, Rust, C) use the default `braces` family, and an indentation language like `python` sets `__grammar__=python`, so it reflects its own symbols and blocks too, not just tokens.

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/comby.cmk', github=github)}}

